WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:01.520
- This episode of LegalEagle
(gentle music)

00:00:01.520 --> 00:00:03.110
was made possible by Skillshare.

00:00:03.110 --> 00:00:05.580
Learn to think like a lawyer
for free for two months

00:00:05.580 --> 00:00:07.730
by clicking on the link
in the description.

00:00:08.570 --> 00:00:10.310
Recent events have raised the question

00:00:10.310 --> 00:00:12.540
of whether a sitting
president can be indicted

00:00:12.540 --> 00:00:15.320
while in office or whether
the president is immune

00:00:15.320 --> 00:00:17.130
from criminal prosecution.

00:00:17.130 --> 00:00:19.150
It has never happened before.

00:00:19.150 --> 00:00:21.710
The text of the Constitution is unclear

00:00:21.710 --> 00:00:24.140
and there are good
arguments on both sides.

00:00:24.140 --> 00:00:26.910
Welcome to Lawsplainer, a
new series on LegalEagle

00:00:26.910 --> 00:00:30.120
where I try to tackle some
of the core legal concepts

00:00:30.120 --> 00:00:32.810
that form the foundation of our society

00:00:32.810 --> 00:00:35.310
because sometimes the law is complicated

00:00:35.310 --> 00:00:37.067
and needs to be 'splained.

00:00:39.143 --> 00:00:41.980
(gavel thuds)

00:00:41.980 --> 00:00:44.690
Today we are talking about
whether a sitting president

00:00:44.690 --> 00:00:46.430
can be criminally indicted.

00:00:46.430 --> 00:00:48.480
This is a legal concept
that becomes relevant

00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:51.660
every 20 years of so starting
with President Nixon,

00:00:51.660 --> 00:00:53.120
moving on to President Clinton,

00:00:53.120 --> 00:00:55.150
and now potentially President Trump.

00:00:55.150 --> 00:00:56.370
But first let's talk about the difference

00:00:56.370 --> 00:00:58.710
between indictment and impeachment.

00:00:58.710 --> 00:01:02.460
Indictment is a criminal
accusation against an individual.

00:01:02.460 --> 00:01:05.510
It is essentially the way you
start a criminal prosecution.

00:01:05.510 --> 00:01:08.340
So for example, if someone
murdered another person,

00:01:08.340 --> 00:01:11.270
the state would indict
that person for murder.

00:01:11.270 --> 00:01:14.620
However impeachment is a
purely political mechanism

00:01:14.620 --> 00:01:18.110
used to remove and official
from the federal government.

00:01:18.110 --> 00:01:20.480
It is a formal charge of wrongdoing

00:01:20.480 --> 00:01:22.830
and potentially removing them from office.

00:01:22.830 --> 00:01:26.010
But unlike an indictment, it
does not necessarily result

00:01:26.010 --> 00:01:27.180
in jail time.

00:01:27.180 --> 00:01:29.530
In popular parlance,
impeachment generally refers

00:01:29.530 --> 00:01:32.180
to the president but it can
actually refer to a wide variety

00:01:32.180 --> 00:01:33.850
of federal government employees.

00:01:33.850 --> 00:01:36.010
In fact, most of the impeachments

00:01:36.010 --> 00:01:39.330
that have actually occurred
involve Supreme Court justices

00:01:39.330 --> 00:01:41.580
not just the President
of the United States.

00:01:41.580 --> 00:01:43.330
So you might ask, why would anyone think

00:01:43.330 --> 00:01:46.530
that the president is
immune from prosecution?

00:01:46.530 --> 00:01:49.020
Well, first let's recognize
that most government officials

00:01:49.020 --> 00:01:51.580
are immune from suit
in order to allow them

00:01:51.580 --> 00:01:54.400
to focus on their jobs
as elected officials.

00:01:54.400 --> 00:01:56.290
For example, the doctrine
of qualified immunity

00:01:56.290 --> 00:01:58.370
protects officials from
facing criminal charges

00:01:58.370 --> 00:02:01.293
or lawsuits for their actions
as long as their actions

00:02:01.293 --> 00:02:04.120
are within the course
and scope of their jobs.

00:02:04.120 --> 00:02:06.760
Qualified immunity is often
a defense that gets raised

00:02:06.760 --> 00:02:10.600
in suits against police
officers or police departments.

00:02:10.600 --> 00:02:13.080
Additionally, looking at the
text of the Constitution,

00:02:13.080 --> 00:02:15.250
you'll notice that it
does mention impeachment

00:02:15.250 --> 00:02:17.950
of the president, but it
does not mention indictment.

00:02:17.950 --> 00:02:20.630
However, the absence of that statement

00:02:20.630 --> 00:02:22.740
does not necessarily mean
that it's not available.

00:02:22.740 --> 00:02:25.420
So there's ambiguity in the Constitution.

00:02:25.420 --> 00:02:27.420
Which is why the Office of Legal Counsel

00:02:27.420 --> 00:02:30.520
has dealt with this topic on
a few different occasions.

00:02:30.520 --> 00:02:33.410
The Office of Legal Counsel, or OLC,

00:02:33.410 --> 00:02:35.730
is part of the United
States Justice Department

00:02:35.730 --> 00:02:38.220
that acts as a legal
advisor to the president

00:02:38.220 --> 00:02:40.250
and the executive branch
when they have questions

00:02:40.250 --> 00:02:43.400
about the legality of a
particular action or law.

00:02:43.400 --> 00:02:46.520
Generally it's seen as
a non-partisan office.

00:02:46.520 --> 00:02:48.580
When the president has a question of law,

00:02:48.580 --> 00:02:52.610
he or she turns to the OLC
to give an objective opinion.

00:02:52.610 --> 00:02:55.360
Relevant here, the OLC
has written three memos

00:02:55.360 --> 00:02:57.290
on the question of whether the president

00:02:57.290 --> 00:02:59.370
can be criminally indicted.

00:02:59.370 --> 00:03:02.400
The first OLC memo was issued in 1973

00:03:02.400 --> 00:03:04.640
shortly before the Saturday Night Massacre

00:03:04.640 --> 00:03:07.180
in which Nixon fired
Watergate Special Prosecutor

00:03:07.180 --> 00:03:09.750
Archibald Cox and the
top two Justice officials

00:03:09.750 --> 00:03:11.520
resigned in protest.

00:03:11.520 --> 00:03:13.610
That memo considers
the Constitution's text

00:03:13.610 --> 00:03:15.350
and finds no answer.

00:03:15.350 --> 00:03:16.940
It finds that the Constitution provides

00:03:16.940 --> 00:03:19.240
very limited immunities
for members of Congress

00:03:19.240 --> 00:03:20.990
and none for the president.

00:03:20.990 --> 00:03:23.590
With no clear constitutional answer,

00:03:23.590 --> 00:03:25.440
the OLC lawyers asked what answer

00:03:25.440 --> 00:03:27.180
would best serve the nation?

00:03:27.180 --> 00:03:29.740
They found that an indictment
in office would besmirch

00:03:29.740 --> 00:03:32.580
the quote symbolic head of the nation

00:03:32.580 --> 00:03:34.650
and given that impeachment
is the only tool mentioned

00:03:34.650 --> 00:03:36.640
in the Constitution, the memo finds

00:03:36.640 --> 00:03:39.340
that this would suggest strongly
that criminal proceedings

00:03:39.340 --> 00:03:42.190
against a president in
office should not go beyond

00:03:42.190 --> 00:03:44.590
the point that would
effectively remove the president

00:03:44.590 --> 00:03:47.950
and thus become a shortcut to impeachment.

00:03:47.950 --> 00:03:50.250
A second memo written
just a few days later

00:03:50.250 --> 00:03:52.690
by Robert Bork argues
that the same points apply

00:03:52.690 --> 00:03:54.290
to the vice president as well.

00:03:54.290 --> 00:03:56.370
Of course, those memos were never tested

00:03:56.370 --> 00:03:59.350
as President Nixon resigned
before either impeachment

00:03:59.350 --> 00:04:02.400
or indictment and was
pardoned by President Ford.

00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:04.630
27 years later in the year 2000,

00:04:04.630 --> 00:04:06.910
the OLC reexamined the question.

00:04:06.910 --> 00:04:09.340
By this time there had been
major Supreme Court decisions,

00:04:09.340 --> 00:04:11.160
which we'll talk about in a second,

00:04:11.160 --> 00:04:12.870
all indicating that the
president was, in fact,

00:04:12.870 --> 00:04:15.790
not immune to the reach
of the justice system.

00:04:15.790 --> 00:04:18.200
But the OLC found that despite case law,

00:04:18.200 --> 00:04:21.330
the president was still immune
from criminal prosecution.

00:04:21.330 --> 00:04:23.590
The OLC memo from the year 2000 states

00:04:23.590 --> 00:04:26.240
that quote, the framers
considered who should possess

00:04:26.240 --> 00:04:27.990
the extraordinary power of deciding

00:04:27.990 --> 00:04:29.320
whether to initiate proceeding

00:04:29.320 --> 00:04:30.680
that could remove the president

00:04:30.680 --> 00:04:32.340
and placed that responsibility

00:04:32.340 --> 00:04:35.100
in the elected officials of Congress.

00:04:35.100 --> 00:04:36.360
It would be inconsistent

00:04:36.360 --> 00:04:38.190
with that carefully considered judgment

00:04:38.190 --> 00:04:40.740
to permit an unelected
grand jury and prosecutor

00:04:40.740 --> 00:04:43.180
to effectively remove a president

00:04:43.180 --> 00:04:45.240
by bringing criminal charges against him

00:04:45.240 --> 00:04:47.380
while he remains in office.

00:04:47.380 --> 00:04:50.080
So again the OLC concluded
that a sitting president

00:04:50.080 --> 00:04:51.940
could not be criminally indicted.

00:04:51.940 --> 00:04:53.640
However, unbeknownst to the public,

00:04:53.640 --> 00:04:57.350
two years earlier in 1998, a secret memo

00:04:57.350 --> 00:04:59.620
written for Independent
Counsel Kenneth Starr

00:04:59.620 --> 00:05:01.460
reached the opposite conclusion.

00:05:01.460 --> 00:05:03.350
At the height of the Starr investigation

00:05:03.350 --> 00:05:06.140
and the Lewinsky scandal,
Independent Counsel Kenneth Starr

00:05:06.140 --> 00:05:09.820
commissioned a legal memo
seeking guidance on this question

00:05:09.820 --> 00:05:12.600
as to whether a sitting
president can be indicted.

00:05:12.600 --> 00:05:14.740
Mr. Starr assigned Ronald Rotunda,

00:05:14.740 --> 00:05:16.610
a prominent Conservative professor

00:05:16.610 --> 00:05:18.540
of constitutional law to write this memo

00:05:18.540 --> 00:05:19.630
as a consultant.

00:05:19.630 --> 00:05:21.600
The memo has only recently been revealed

00:05:21.600 --> 00:05:24.750
after a FOIA request
by The New York Times.

00:05:24.750 --> 00:05:27.750
The Starr memo argues that
President Clinton at the time

00:05:27.750 --> 00:05:30.710
could be indicted because
quote, the US Supreme Court

00:05:30.710 --> 00:05:33.150
has repeatedly reaffirmed the statement

00:05:33.150 --> 00:05:35.410
that no one is above the law.

00:05:35.410 --> 00:05:38.810
The memo argued that the
Independent Counsel's statue itself

00:05:38.810 --> 00:05:41.120
made no sense if the
Counsel could not, in fact,

00:05:41.120 --> 00:05:42.450
indict the president.

00:05:42.450 --> 00:05:45.240
And President Clinton
had consented to passage

00:05:45.240 --> 00:05:47.210
of the statute that created the office.

00:05:47.210 --> 00:05:50.610
So, in effect, he had
consented to being indicted.

00:05:50.610 --> 00:05:52.620
The Starr memo argued that it is quote,

00:05:52.620 --> 00:05:56.220
proper and constitutional and
legal for a federal grand jury

00:05:56.220 --> 00:05:59.300
to indict a sitting president
for serious criminal acts

00:05:59.300 --> 00:06:01.810
that are not part of and are contrary to

00:06:01.810 --> 00:06:03.980
the president's official duties.

00:06:03.980 --> 00:06:06.020
The Starr office memo concludes by saying

00:06:06.020 --> 00:06:09.160
that in this country, no
one, even President Clinton,

00:06:09.160 --> 00:06:10.540
is above the law.

00:06:10.540 --> 00:06:13.360
So in terms of executive branch memoranda,

00:06:13.360 --> 00:06:15.310
there have been three memoranda that say

00:06:15.310 --> 00:06:17.880
that the president cannot
be criminally indicted,

00:06:17.880 --> 00:06:19.300
at least not while in office

00:06:19.300 --> 00:06:21.570
and one that says the
president can, in fact,

00:06:21.570 --> 00:06:24.000
be indicted while
sitting as the president.

00:06:24.000 --> 00:06:26.060
But you should probably consider the bias

00:06:26.060 --> 00:06:27.850
of these particular sources.

00:06:27.850 --> 00:06:29.730
The three no-indictment
opinions were issued

00:06:29.730 --> 00:06:32.270
by executive branch lawyers who, in fact,

00:06:32.270 --> 00:06:33.760
work for the president.

00:06:33.760 --> 00:06:35.700
And the one legal memorandum that argues

00:06:35.700 --> 00:06:38.600
that the president can be
indicted came specifically

00:06:38.600 --> 00:06:40.470
at the request of the famously,

00:06:40.470 --> 00:06:43.710
perhaps infamously, zealous Kenneth Starr.

00:06:43.710 --> 00:06:46.100
Of course, it has been noted
that the Department of Justice

00:06:46.100 --> 00:06:50.210
has an active policy of not
indicting a sitting president.

00:06:50.210 --> 00:06:52.370
However, this is a policy preference

00:06:52.370 --> 00:06:54.630
of the Department of
Justice and no one argues

00:06:54.630 --> 00:06:57.880
that it is mandated by the
Constitution or statute.

00:06:57.880 --> 00:07:00.760
It may or may not be a good
idea but there is no law

00:07:00.760 --> 00:07:03.620
that requires this particular DOJ policy.

00:07:03.620 --> 00:07:06.270
And this DOJ policy and those memos

00:07:06.270 --> 00:07:09.660
from the Office of Legal
Counsel may be well-reasoned

00:07:09.660 --> 00:07:11.780
but none of them are binding.

00:07:11.780 --> 00:07:14.250
The final answer may
come to us specifically

00:07:14.250 --> 00:07:17.050
from the Supreme Court
of the United States.

00:07:17.050 --> 00:07:18.760
And if we're talking
about the Supreme Court,

00:07:18.760 --> 00:07:21.450
we should look at past
Supreme Court precedent.

00:07:21.450 --> 00:07:24.620
There are two Supreme Court
cases that are on point.

00:07:24.620 --> 00:07:26.750
The first is US versus Nixon.

00:07:26.750 --> 00:07:29.280
In that case, Nixon argued
that he need not turn over

00:07:29.280 --> 00:07:31.390
what became the famous Nixon tapes,

00:07:31.390 --> 00:07:34.430
the smoking gun of the
whole Watergate affair.

00:07:34.430 --> 00:07:37.300
In US versus Nixon, the
Supreme Court confirmed

00:07:37.300 --> 00:07:39.540
that the president is
susceptible to discovery

00:07:39.540 --> 00:07:42.590
that might result in criminal
charges against the executive.

00:07:42.590 --> 00:07:45.030
The Supreme Court rejected
President Nixon's claim

00:07:45.030 --> 00:07:48.730
to a quote, absolute, unqualified
presidential privilege

00:07:48.730 --> 00:07:52.580
of immunity from judicial
process under all circumstances.

00:07:52.580 --> 00:07:54.790
US versus Nixon required
that the president

00:07:54.790 --> 00:07:57.920
turn over his tapes to a
court seeking to use them

00:07:57.920 --> 00:08:00.720
in a criminal case
against other defendants.

00:08:00.720 --> 00:08:04.240
This was a unanimous decision of 8-0

00:08:04.240 --> 00:08:06.260
with Justice Rehnquist recusing himself

00:08:06.260 --> 00:08:08.950
because of his close ties
to the Nixon administration.

00:08:08.950 --> 00:08:10.830
The next relevant Supreme Court case

00:08:10.830 --> 00:08:14.350
came about 20 years later
in Clinton versus Jones.

00:08:14.350 --> 00:08:18.330
In 1994, Paula Jones filed a
civil sexual harassment suit

00:08:18.330 --> 00:08:20.140
against President Clinton.

00:08:20.140 --> 00:08:22.400
Although the allegations
related to Clinton's conduct

00:08:22.400 --> 00:08:25.380
as Governor of Arkansas,
Jones filed her suit

00:08:25.380 --> 00:08:29.320
about five months after Clinton
took office as president.

00:08:29.320 --> 00:08:32.210
Although Clinton, like Nixon,
claimed absolute immunity

00:08:32.210 --> 00:08:34.230
from legal process while in office,

00:08:34.230 --> 00:08:37.150
the Court again rejected that claim.

00:08:37.150 --> 00:08:39.040
The Supreme Court held unanimously

00:08:39.040 --> 00:08:41.270
that President Bill Clinton could be sued

00:08:41.270 --> 00:08:43.550
in civil court over the sexual advances

00:08:43.550 --> 00:08:47.000
he allegedly made while
being Governor of Arkansas.

00:08:47.000 --> 00:08:49.830
Clinton's lawyers, like
Nixon's before him,

00:08:49.830 --> 00:08:52.270
argue that the president's
participation in a trial

00:08:52.270 --> 00:08:54.200
would imperil the executive branch

00:08:54.200 --> 00:08:56.640
because it would distract
him from all the complex

00:08:56.640 --> 00:08:59.710
and numerous responsibilities
of being president.

00:08:59.710 --> 00:09:01.730
But the Court decided that this principle

00:09:01.730 --> 00:09:03.780
was outweighed by the
requirement that litigants

00:09:03.780 --> 00:09:06.850
be able to seek justice
when they are wronged.

00:09:06.850 --> 00:09:09.460
Although Clinton v Jones
involved a civil case

00:09:09.460 --> 00:09:12.380
and not a criminal case,
there's probably no reason

00:09:12.380 --> 00:09:15.530
why the Court's logic can't
extend to criminal cases.

00:09:15.530 --> 00:09:17.690
Indeed you could argue that
the interest of justice

00:09:17.690 --> 00:09:20.350
would be even more
important in a criminal case

00:09:20.350 --> 00:09:23.060
as opposed to a civil case for damages.

00:09:23.060 --> 00:09:25.430
Like many issues in
constitutional jurisprudence,

00:09:25.430 --> 00:09:28.120
there are no easy answers
to this particular question.

00:09:28.120 --> 00:09:30.520
And when lawyers don't
have binding precedent,

00:09:30.520 --> 00:09:32.520
they make what are
called policy arguments.

00:09:32.520 --> 00:09:35.100
In other words, in cases
of first impression,

00:09:35.100 --> 00:09:37.760
they argue what would make the best law?

00:09:37.760 --> 00:09:41.430
So let's look at a few of
those arguments on both sides.

00:09:41.430 --> 00:09:43.460
Those arguing for immunity from indictment

00:09:43.460 --> 00:09:46.890
would claim that the president
is singularly important.

00:09:46.890 --> 00:09:49.180
They would argue that the
president can't be distracted

00:09:49.180 --> 00:09:52.030
by civil and criminal
suits that would prevent

00:09:52.030 --> 00:09:54.070
the president from turning
his or her attention

00:09:54.070 --> 00:09:55.800
to actually running the country.

00:09:55.800 --> 00:09:57.190
And, in fact, this was the basis

00:09:57.190 --> 00:09:59.080
of Justice Kavanaugh's Law Review piece

00:09:59.080 --> 00:10:01.750
that became relevant during
his confirmation hearings.

00:10:01.750 --> 00:10:03.530
Kavanaugh argued that
because the president

00:10:03.530 --> 00:10:05.830
is so important, Congress
should pass a law

00:10:05.830 --> 00:10:08.720
that makes the president
exempt from investigations,

00:10:08.720 --> 00:10:10.840
exactly the kind of
investigations that Kavanaugh

00:10:10.840 --> 00:10:13.740
helped run during the Starr investigation.

00:10:13.740 --> 00:10:16.260
However, Kavanaugh doesn't
argue that this is mandated

00:10:16.260 --> 00:10:19.560
by the Constitution, simply
that Congress should, in fact,

00:10:19.560 --> 00:10:20.880
pass that law.

00:10:20.880 --> 00:10:24.210
Proponents of immunity would
also argue logistical problems.

00:10:24.210 --> 00:10:26.450
The president is the
head of the Executive.

00:10:26.450 --> 00:10:29.190
So how can the president
effectively oversee

00:10:29.190 --> 00:10:31.800
his or her own trial and investigation

00:10:31.800 --> 00:10:34.110
and potential criminal conviction?

00:10:34.110 --> 00:10:34.943
They would also argue

00:10:34.943 --> 00:10:37.340
that we already have
a remedy, impeachment.

00:10:37.340 --> 00:10:40.450
If the president has done
some sort of wrongdoing,

00:10:40.450 --> 00:10:43.450
it's up to Congress in order
to reprimand the president

00:10:43.450 --> 00:10:45.120
and remove them from office.

00:10:45.120 --> 00:10:46.730
And once they are removed from office,

00:10:46.730 --> 00:10:48.320
then prosecute any crimes

00:10:48.320 --> 00:10:50.010
that may or may not have occurred.

00:10:50.010 --> 00:10:53.000
But I think even those that
would argue for immunity,

00:10:53.000 --> 00:10:55.730
would agree that if indeed
the president is immune

00:10:55.730 --> 00:10:57.540
from criminal prosecution of events

00:10:57.540 --> 00:10:59.610
that occurred before
they became president,

00:10:59.610 --> 00:11:00.880
that the statute of limitations

00:11:00.880 --> 00:11:02.560
must be tolled while they're in office

00:11:02.560 --> 00:11:05.320
otherwise becoming
president literally would be

00:11:05.320 --> 00:11:07.230
a get-out-of-jail-free card.

00:11:07.230 --> 00:11:09.040
On the other hand, those who would argue

00:11:09.040 --> 00:11:12.010
against presidential immunity
and those who would argue

00:11:12.010 --> 00:11:14.200
that, in fact, a
president can be indicted,

00:11:14.200 --> 00:11:16.460
would point out that pre-election conduct

00:11:16.460 --> 00:11:20.800
is by definition not related
to their job as president.

00:11:20.800 --> 00:11:23.060
No official should be above the law.

00:11:23.060 --> 00:11:26.090
It's a principle that is
reaffirmed time and time again

00:11:26.090 --> 00:11:27.250
from the Supreme Court.

00:11:27.250 --> 00:11:29.710
And to borrow an example
that President Trump uses

00:11:29.710 --> 00:11:30.730
from time to time--

00:11:30.730 --> 00:11:31.850
- I have the most loyal people.

00:11:31.850 --> 00:11:33.170
Did you ever see that?

00:11:33.170 --> 00:11:35.120
Where I could stand in
the middle of 5th Avenue

00:11:35.120 --> 00:11:37.689
and shoot somebody and I
wouldn't lose any voters, okay?

00:11:37.689 --> 00:11:39.003
(audience laughing)
It's like incredible.

00:11:40.120 --> 00:11:42.750
- If he shot a man in
cold blood on 5th Avenue,

00:11:42.750 --> 00:11:45.360
perhaps his followers might not care,

00:11:45.360 --> 00:11:48.050
but I think as a society
we would probably want

00:11:48.050 --> 00:11:50.870
the president prosecuted for that crime.

00:11:50.870 --> 00:11:51.810
This issue is sort of

00:11:51.810 --> 00:11:54.740
like the Schrodinger's cat of politics.

00:11:54.740 --> 00:11:56.330
As with many things in the law,

00:11:56.330 --> 00:11:59.300
we may not have an answer
until the actual action

00:11:59.300 --> 00:12:03.190
in question is taken and the
Supreme Court issues a decision

00:12:03.190 --> 00:12:05.310
that finally tells us
one way or the other.

00:12:05.310 --> 00:12:07.910
Until then, in the absence
of a law passed by Congress

00:12:07.910 --> 00:12:09.500
or a constitutional amendment,

00:12:09.500 --> 00:12:11.920
we may never know the
answer to this question.

00:12:11.920 --> 00:12:13.790
